# Forms of `get` are left out on purpose. Matching is case-insensitive,
# so `Get returns the value` in a Go doc comment would trip a `get VERB`
# branch, and Harper's tagger rarely marks the participle after `gets`
# anyway (`gets flushed` goes unflagged either way).
#
# The filter `<VERB, !@stative>` requires one token to be a verb and
# not one of the stative words the tagger also marks as verbs: a
# progressive (`has been running` is active voice), the participial
# adjectives docs lean on (`the flag is set`, `is enabled by default`,
# `the port is stuck`), and the naming idioms (`is called name`, `is
# known as a handle`, `is meant for testing`).
#
# The `by` branch exists because the tagger misses many participles
# (`is guarded by a lock`). It accepts any word the tagger does not
# mark as an adjective, adverb, noun, or preposition, so a degree (`off
# by one`) and a deadline (`empty by the time`) pass. Weir has no token
# class for a number, so the object of `by` must be a determiner,
# pronoun, or proper noun instead: that skips a dimension (`64 by 64`),
# a criterion (`keyed by path`), and the phrases where `by` names no
# actor (`by default`, `by design`, `by hand`), while `by a lock`, `by
# the scheduler`, and `by git` still count as agents.
#
# The tagger marks many participles as adjectives (`cached`,
# `retried`, `flushed`), so `missed` lists the common ones by hand.
# `exported` and `deprecated` stay out, because a Go doc uses each to
# name a state (`the field is exported`, `the method is deprecated`).
# `not` sits beside the adverb slot, since the tagger does not mark it
# as one (`the value is not written`).
#
# The `!-` glued after the verb skips a hyphenated compound whose
# first half the tagger marks as a verb (`is read-only`, `are
# fail-open`). It matches at the end of a clause too, so `the file is
# skipped.` still counts.
#
# A stative word still reads as a passive when an agent follows it
# (`the flag is set by the caller`, `the binary is built by the
# pipeline`), so `agentive` lists the stative words that take one.
# The agent must start with a determiner, pronoun, or proper noun, as
# in the `by` branch, and the determiner may not open `by the time`
# or `by the way`.
#
# `being` takes any participle but `said`, since `That being said` is
# the opener the throat-clearing rule reports. The first branch takes
# every other form of `be` through the filter `<@be, !being>`.
#
# Two shapes stay flagged on purpose: an identifier used as a predicate
# noun (`The default is print`), which belongs in a code span, and a
# resultative participle (`The issue is closed`), which the tagger
# cannot tell from the event and which the skill rewrites anyway.
expr stative [PROG, missing, done, set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, available, stuck, broken, gone, finished, lost, left, known, frozen, wedged, based, tied, built, made, fixed, called, named, meant, supposed, bound, referred, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned]
expr missed [cached, compiled, computed, configured, decoded, discarded, encoded, evicted, fetched, flushed, hashed, linted, normalized, parsed, patched, persisted, propagated, queued, redirected, reset, restarted, retried, revoked, rotated, sanitized, serialized, stripped, synced, tagged, truncated, validated]
expr be [is, are, was, were, be, been, being, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't]
expr verb [<VERB, !@stative>, @missed]
expr agentive [set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, broken, known, built, made, fixed, called, named, bound, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned]
expr agent [(DET ![time, way]), PRON, PROPN]
expr main [(<@be, !being> [@verb, ([ADV, not] @verb)]!-), (being [<@verb, !said>, ([ADV, not] @verb)]!-), (@be <!@stative, !ADJ, !ADV, !NOUN, !ADP, !DET, !PRON, !INTJ> by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (@be [@agentive, ([ADV, not] @agentive)] by @agent)]
let message "Passive voice. Make the actor the subject."
let description "Flags a form of be followed by a verb, and a form of be followed by a word and by an agent."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The scheduler retries the job."
allows "The scheduler is retrying the job."
allows "The job has been running for an hour."
allows "She is tired."
allows "The lock is free."
allows "There is a lock."
allows "Get returns the value stored under key."
allows "The flag is enabled by default."
allows "When the flag is set, the hook skips the file."
allows "The file is missing."
allows "The count is off by one."
allows "The queue is empty by the time the loop runs."
allows "The image is 64 by 64 pixels."
allows "The two panes are side by side."
allows "The output is stable by design."
allows "The mapping is done by hand."
allows "The migration is done by Friday."
allows "The field is called name."
allows "The type is known as a handle."
allows "The flag is meant for testing."
allows "The field is exported."
allows "The method is deprecated."
allows "The port is stuck in CLOSE_WAIT."
allows "Be warned that the flag is sticky."
allows "The default is lookup by id."
allows "The answer is yes by the way."
allows "The flag is set by the time the hook runs."
allows "The value is set by hand."
allows "The lock is read-only while the sweep runs."
allows "Reads are fail-open and writes are fail-closed."
allows "That being said, the hook skips the file."
